commons orders.stock

---
A stock-keeping unit: three capital letters, a dash, four digits.

The pattern is part of the type, so a function that takes a `Sku` never
has to ask whether the string is well formed.
---
type Sku = String where Matches("^[A-Z]{3}-[0-9]{4}$")

--- How many of one item a single order line may ask for.
type Quantity = Int where InRange(1, 99)

---
One line of an order.

Both fields are refined, so a `Line` that exists is already valid. This is
also the shape of the HTTP body: the boundary checks it before any handler runs.
---
type Line = {
	sku: Sku,
	qty: Quantity,
}

--- What one unit of any item costs, in pence.
const UnitPence: Int = 250

---
The quantity a new line starts with.

`1` is a literal in a `Quantity` position, so the compiler checks it against
`InRange(1, 99)` and admits it: no `.of`, no `Result`. Writing `0` here
would be a compile error.
---
fn firstQuantity() -> Quantity {
	1
}

---
The price of a line, in pence.

No validation here: a `Quantity` is between 1 and 99 by construction.
---
fn linePrice(line: Line) -> Int {
	line.qty * UnitPence
}

---
The same line with one more unit.

Arithmetic on a refined value yields its base type, so `line.qty + 1` is a
plain `Int` that may be 100. It has to go back through `Quantity.of`, which
returns a `Result` the caller must handle.
---
fn addOne(line: Line) -> Result[Line, ValidationError] {
	let qty = Quantity.of(line.qty + 1)?
	Ok(Line { sku: line.sku, qty: qty })
}
